{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "There is an increasing body of evidence demonstrating that the accuracy of PoCUS is comparable to the current imaging reference standard CXR in general [16] as well as in specific conditions, such as pneumonia [17], acute decompensated heart failure [16], pleural effusion [18], pneumothorax [19] and pulmonary embolism [20]. PoCUS has other advantages, such as being free from ionizing radiation, and most importantly can be performed in real-time at the bedside [16, 21]. Additionally, PoCUS can answer a broad spectrum of remaining diagnostic questions and may also help to optimize and personalize therapy [22]. However, very few trials have examined meaningful clinical outcomes related to PoCUS usage to date [23] and the results on outcome measurements were heterogeneous [24].",
      "reason": "The manuscript summarizes the current evidence on PoCUS diagnostic accuracy, its advantages, and explicitly notes gaps/uncertainties (few trials on clinical outcomes and heterogeneous results)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Acute-onset dyspnea is one of the most common symptoms for which patients visit the Emergency Department (ED) [1-4]. In the United States, dyspnea is the main reason for four to five million ED visits annually [4], representing up to 50% of patients admitted to acute tertiary care hospitals [5].... In addition to its high incidence, the 30-day mortality rate of these patients remains relatively high (8-13%) [7, 8]. Therefore, rapid and appropriate diagnosis of the underlying pathology is of utmost importance for prompt and adequate treatment [9]. ... Therefore, we conducted a high-quality, comprehensive systematic review and meta-analysis that included the most recent publications that reported clinical outcomes with the use of PoCUS in patients who developed acute onset dyspnea.",
      "reason": "The introduction states the high incidence and mortality of dyspnea and explains the need for rapid diagnosis, justifying why the review was conducted to assess PoCUS effects on clinical outcomes."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_19",
      "quote": "In contrast to previous reviews and meta-analyses [16-20, 60] that analyzed data from patients with an explicit diagnosis, such as pneumonia or acute decompensated heart failure [9, 10, 16, 17, 62], we applied a broader definition of dyspnea, thereby including more patients and providing more comprehensive results.",
      "reason": "The authors contrast their review with previous reviews and explain the need for and added value of their broader approach. Additional support in text_21 notes prior reviews and that this review reports ED and ICU LOS as novel outcomes."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "PoCUS has other advantages, such as being free from ionizing radiation, and most importantly can be performed in real-time at the bedside [16, 21]. Additionally, PoCUS can answer a broad spectrum of remaining diagnostic questions and may also help to optimize and personalize therapy [22].",
      "reason": "The manuscript briefly describes how PoCUS works in practice (real-time bedside imaging, diagnostic breadth) and how that can lead to optimized/personalized therapy—constituting a brief proposed mechanism of action for the intervention."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence (text or figure) was provided of a logic model or schematic showing hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "The aim of this study was to investigate the effects of the use of Point-of-Care Ultrasound (PoCUS) versus conventional management on clinical outcomes in patients with acute onset dyspnea.",
      "reason": "The abstract provides an explicit statement of the review objective comparing PoCUS versus conventional management on clinical outcomes in patients with acute onset dyspnea; the Introduction similarly states the main objective to investigate how PoCUS improves clinical endpoints."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The selected studies had to match our previously defined PICO (Patients, Intervention, Control, Outcome) framework: ... P: Adults and children who were admitted to the ED or to the Intensive Care Unit (ICU), or to another inpatient setting because of acute onset or worsening dyspnea ... I: The examined intervention was PoCUS use on its own or in combination with conventional diagnostic measures. ... C: Control group included conventional diagnostic methods, such as taking the patients' medical history, physical examination, ECG, blood gas and different laboratory analyses, echocardiography, CXR, or computer tomography (CT).",
      "reason": "The review explicitly states the planned comparisons using a PICO framework with detailed descriptions of Patients, Intervention, and Control (and Outcomes elsewhere), satisfying the conditional requirement for intervention reviews."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The selected studies had to match our previously defined PICO (Patients, Intervention, Control, Outcome) framework: ... P: Adults and children who were admitted to the ED or to the Intensive Care Unit (ICU), or to another inpatient setting because of acute onset or worsening dyspnea were eligible. We also included studies enrolling patients who developed shortness of breath from unknown etiologies and were already hospitalized. Studies reporting on trauma-induced acute onset dyspnea, or pregnancy were excluded. ... I: The examined intervention was PoCUS use on its own or in combination with conventional diagnostic measures. ... There were no restrictions on the type of PoCUS protocols. ... C: Control group included conventional diagnostic methods, such as taking the patients' medical history, physical examination, ECG, blood gas and different laboratory analyses, echocardiography, CXR, or computer tomography (CT).",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies PICO components (patients, intervention, control), eligible settings (ED, ICU, inpatient), populations included/excluded (e.g., trauma-induced dyspnea, pregnancy excluded), intervention details (PoCUS on its own or with conventional measures) and control definitions, satisfying specification of study characteristics used to determine eligibility."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "We did not use any restrictions or limitations based on language or publication date.",
      "reason": "The review explicitly states no restrictions on language or publication date. Additionally, selection criteria (text_6) exclude conference abstracts and non-peer-reviewed articles, indicating publication/report status restrictions were specified (e.g., exclusion of conference abstracts and non-peer-reviewed articles)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No information was provided distinguishing exclusion because outcomes were not measured from exclusion because outcome results were not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "C: Control group included conventional diagnostic methods, such as taking the patients' medical history, physical examination, ECG, blood gas and different laboratory analyses, echocardiography, CXR, or computer tomography (CT).",
      "reason": "The review defines the groups used in syntheses (PoCUS intervention versus conventional diagnostic methods as control) and links them to comparisons evaluated in the review objectives."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "Although some eligibility restrictions are listed (e.g., excluding trauma-induced dyspnea, pregnancy, certain study types), the manuscript does not provide explicit rationales explaining why these notable restrictions were applied."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "75"
}

